INSERT INTO "intel" VALUES('guild','The riddler''s guild.
INSERT INTO "intel" VALUES('latest','Latest intel regarding case M
INSERT INTO "intel" VALUES('h','Yours truly, the architect of this database, watson and most of these tools. Quite frankly, I’m brilliant, but I can only do so much.');
INSERT INTO "intel" VALUES('watson','Watson is a bot; nothing more than a bundle of scripts. I’m quite prone to anthropomorphising him, though, and it amuses me to treat him as both a ‘he’ and as a true assistant. In reality, he’s merely a useful tool to provide quick access to some cryptographic operations.');
INSERT INTO "intel" VALUES('m','M’ is the codename assigned to the unknown criminal responsible for a wave of recent major thefts and terrorist activities. His identity is unknown, the size and resourcing of his organisation is unknown. His motivations are unknown. He makes heavy use of cryptography, information warfare techniques and is very, very careful. We see him by his actions only, and he’s yet to cast enough of a shadow to reveal who he really is in this world.');
INSERT INTO "intel" VALUES('cipher','A cipher is a general term for a process that allows encryption or decryption of data. Somewhat of a hobby of mine, they range from the simple [[b;;]substitution] ciphers to modern-day [[b;;]asymmetric] key algorithims.');
INSERT INTO "intel" VALUES('substitution','A substitution cipher is a simple beast. One bit of data is exchanged for another, in a predetermined pattern. The case of [[b;;]rot13] is a famously straightforward case of a caesar cipher, but they’re all in this vein: it’s just a matter of how you reorganise the letters you’re working with.');
INSERT INTO "intel" VALUES('rot13','‘rot13’ means ‘rotate thirteen places’. So every alphabetic letter in a message would be exchanged for another, thirteen places along in the alphabet. Naturally, the alphabet ‘loops’ when picking letters, so therefore a [[b;;]plaintext] ‘o’ will become a [[b;;]ciphertext] ‘b’. The general form of this cipher is ‘rotx’, where ‘x’ is the number of places you’re rotating.');
